---
import '../../../styles/global.css';
import model from '../../../../model/dynamos-v2.json';
import derivation from '../../../../model/precepta-derivation.json';

const rawBase = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const base = rawBase.endsWith('/') ? rawBase : `${rawBase}/`;

const counts = derivation.patterns.reduce(
  (result, pattern) => {
    result[pattern.inference_status] = (result[pattern.inference_status] ?? 0) + 1;
    return result;
  },
  {}
);
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="How DYNAMOS derives PRECEPTA patterns from the 26-dimensional architecture, and what is literature-grounded, model-constructed, and still experimental."
    />
    <title>How PRECEPTA Is Derived — DYNAMOS</title>
  </head>

  <body>
    <header class="site-header">
      <a class="wordmark" href={base}>DYNAMOS</a>
      <nav aria-label="Primary navigation">
        <a href={`${base}model/`}>Model</a>
        <a aria-current="page" href={`${base}precepta/`}>PRECEPTA</a>
        <a href={`${base}explore/`}>Explore</a>
        <a href={`${base}research/`}>Research</a>
        <a href={`${base}faq/`}>FAQ</a>
        <a href={`${base}responsible-use/`}>Responsible use</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <main>
      <section class="derivation-hero">
        <div class="breadcrumb">
          <a href={`${base}precepta/`}>PRECEPTA</a>
          <span>→</span>
          <span>Derivation</span>
        </div>

        <p class="eyebrow">Derivation · {derivation.derivation_version}</p>
        <h1>How PRECEPTA is derived.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          The derivation grammar is public because an interpretation should be
          inspectable. These rules formalize current DYNAMOS hypotheses; they are
          not probabilities, diagnoses, or validated psychometric laws.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="two-column">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Origin</p>
          <h2>Start with phenomena, not labels.</h2>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>
            DYNAMOS was developed through a theory-driven, literature-anchored process.
            Research on dynamic systems, attention and cognitive effort, masking and
            camouflaging, autism, ADHD, giftedness, sensory experience, and related
            neurodiversity literatures supplied recurring phenomena and theoretical
            constraints.
          </p>
          <p>
            Diagnostic categories were not treated as hidden mechanisms or causal ground
            truth. Instead, recurring observations were treated as phenomena to explain:
            what lower-level operating dynamics could produce them, under which contexts,
            at what cost, and over what timescale?
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="development-flow">
        <p class="eyebrow">Development path</p>
        <div class="flow-grid">
          <article><span>01</span><strong>Published phenomena & theory</strong><p>Recurring observations and theoretical constraints enter as source material.</p></article>
          <article><span>02</span><strong>Mechanisms</strong><p>Diagnostic wording is stripped away and lower-level operating dynamics are proposed.</p></article>
          <article><span>03</span><strong>26 dimensions</strong><p>The mechanisms are represented as continuous architectural dimensions.</p></article>
          <article><span>04</span><strong>PRECEPTA</strong><p>Combinations are translated into recognizable, human-readable patterns.</p></article>
          <article><span>05</span><strong>Formal rules</strong><p>Core, supporting, and modifying dimensions are encoded as machine-readable hypotheses.</p></article>
          <article><span>06</span><strong>Empirical feedback</strong><p>Recognition, rejection, uncertainty, and missing patterns become evidence for revision.</p></article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="evidence-boundary">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Evidence boundary</p>
          <h2>Literature-grounded does not mean every signature is literature-validated.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="boundary-grid">
          <article>
            <strong>Theoretical foundations</strong>
            <p>
              Grounded in published work on dynamic systems, cognitive effort,
              compensatory control, masking/camouflaging, and related research traditions.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <strong>26-dimensional architecture</strong>
            <p>
              A theory-motivated DYNAMOS construction. It is designed to operationalize
              those dynamics but is not yet independently psychometrically validated.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <strong>32 PRECEPTA signatures</strong>
            <p>
              DYNAMOS model constructions derived from the architecture and development
              process. They are hypotheses about how combinations may become visible.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <strong>Derivation grammar</strong>
            <p>
              Experimental machine-readable rules for testing those hypotheses. Public
              transparency makes them auditable; empirical validation remains ongoing.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="two-column">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Rule grammar</p>
          <h2>Different dimensions play different roles.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="role-list">
          <article>
            <strong>Core</strong>
            <p>
              Carries the defining mechanism. A pattern is considered only when no core
              condition lies in the opposite score region.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <strong>Supporting</strong>
            <p>
              Strengthens and orders compatible candidates but does not create or veto
              the pattern by itself.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <strong>Modifier</strong>
            <p>
              Changes expression, cost, or sustainability without deciding whether the
              base pattern exists.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <strong>Context or dynamics</strong>
            <p>
              Some patterns depend on a change across situations or time. If the 26 static
              ratings cannot observe that defining feature, DYNAMOS says so rather than
              pretending it was measured.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="status-section">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Inference status</p>
          <h2>Not every PRECEPTA is inferred in the same way.</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="status-grid">
          <article>
            <strong>{counts.inferable ?? 0}</strong>
            <h3>Directly inferable</h3>
            <p>
              The current 26 ratings can test the core architecture. Matching remains
              provisional and does not imply probability or diagnosis.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <strong>{counts.context_sensitive ?? 0}</strong>
            <h3>Context-sensitive</h3>
            <p>
              The static architecture can support the pattern, while a defining
              contextual or dynamic feature remains unobserved.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <strong>{counts.not_directly_inferable ?? 0}</strong>
            <h3>Not directly inferable</h3>
            <p>
              The defining phenomenon is not measured by the 26 ratings. These patterns
              are withheld from automatic inference and may be tested with targeted follow-up.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="two-column">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Degeneracy</p>
          <h2>The same appearance may have different origins.</h2>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>
            PRECEPTA is intentionally many-to-many. More than one architecture may produce
            a similar visible pattern, and one architecture may support several patterns.
            A rule therefore represents a compatible mechanism, not proof that a single
            hidden cause has been discovered.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is also why no single dimension produces a PRECEPTA pattern and why
            context, cost, sustainability, and time remain part of interpretation.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="feedback-section">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Research loop</p>
          <h2>Give the model a chance to be wrong.</h2>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>
            Participant feedback is not a test of whether the participant understood
            themselves correctly. It is evidence about whether the derivation produced
            a useful higher-order interpretation.
          </p>
          <blockquote>
            Disagreement is evidence about the derivation, not participant error.
          </blockquote>
          <p>
            Strong recognition, partial recognition, rejection, uncertainty, and
            structured missing-pattern feedback can all inform later revisions.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="machine-section">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Public specification</p>
          <h2>Inspect the actual rules.</h2>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>
            Current derivation: <strong>{derivation.derivation_version}</strong><br />
            Model: <strong>{derivation.model_version}</strong><br />
            Status: <strong>{derivation.role_assignment_status}</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            <a class="primary-link" href="https://github.com/eebyak/DYNAMOS/blob/main/model/precepta-derivation.json">
              Open machine-readable derivation ↗
            </a>
          </p>
          <p><a class="text-link" href={`${base}precepta/`}>← Back to all PRECEPTA patterns</a></p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>

    <footer>
      <div>
        <p>
          <strong>DYNAMOS — Dynamic Neuro-Operational Systems Model</strong><br />
          © {model.copyright.year} {model.copyright.holder}
        </p>
        <p>Model and content licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0.</p>

        <p class="citation-note">
          <strong>Cite v2.0.0:</strong> Berkling, K. (2026). DYNAMOS: Dynamic Neuro-Operational Systems Model (Version 2.0.0). Zenodo.
          <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22977734">doi:10.5281/zenodo.22977734</a>
          · <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22977733">all versions</a>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="footer-notice">
        <p><strong>Research status:</strong> public derivation hypotheses, not validated psychometric rules.</p>
        <p><a href={`${base}responsible-use/`}>Responsible use →</a></p>
      </div>
    </footer>

    <style>
      .derivation-hero {
        padding: 7rem 2rem 5rem;
      }

      .breadcrumb {
        display: flex;
        gap: 0.65rem;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
        font-size: 0.9rem;
      }

      .two-column,
      .evidence-boundary,
      .feedback-section,
      .machine-section {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: 0.9fr 1.2fr;
        gap: 4rem;
        padding: 5rem 2rem;
        border-top: 1px solid #b9b6ad;
      }

      .two-column h2,
      .evidence-boundary h2,
      .feedback-section h2,
      .machine-section h2 {
        font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3.8rem);
      }

      .development-flow,
      .status-section {
        padding: 5rem 2rem;
        border-top: 1px solid #b9b6ad;
      }

      .flow-grid,
      .boundary-grid,
      .role-list,
      .status-grid {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
        gap: 1rem;
        margin-top: 2rem;
      }

      .flow-grid article,
      .boundary-grid article,
      .role-list article,
      .status-grid article {
        padding: 1.25rem;
        border: 1px solid #d2cfc6;
        border-radius: 0.65rem;
      }

      .flow-grid span {
        display: inline-block;
        margin-bottom: 1rem;
        font-size: 0.78rem;
        font-weight: 800;
        letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      }

      .flow-grid strong,
      .boundary-grid strong,
      .role-list strong {
        display: block;
        font-size: 1.12rem;
      }

      .status-grid {
        grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
      }

      .status-grid > article > strong {
        display: block;
        font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 5vw, 4.5rem);
        line-height: 1;
      }

      .status-grid h3 {
        margin: 0.6rem 0;
        font-size: 1.2rem;
      }

      .feedback-section blockquote {
        margin: 1.5rem 0;
        padding-left: 1.2rem;
        border-left: 3px solid currentColor;
        font-size: 1.25rem;
        line-height: 1.5;
      }

      .primary-link {
        display: inline-block;
        padding: 0.8rem 1rem;
        border: 1px solid #17202a;
        border-radius: 0.55rem;
        background: #17202a;
        color: #f7f5ef;
        text-decoration: none;
        font-weight: 750;
      }

      @media (max-width: 820px) {
        .two-column,
        .evidence-boundary,
        .feedback-section,
        .machine-section,
        .flow-grid,
        .boundary-grid,
        .role-list,
        .status-grid {
          grid-template-columns: 1fr;
        }
      }
    </style>
  </body>
</html>
